  <h2 id="vault"><a class="anchor" href="#vault">#</a>Vault layout</h2>
<pre>hadsed.com/
  about.md
  experience/          (unchanged)
  projects/home-3d.md
  blog/home-3d-from-an-address.md
  notes/plan-fit.md
  assets/              images, and .html files for embeds</pre>
  <p>Filenames are yours to rename in Obsidian. URLs come from front matter, so a rename never moves a page.</p>

  <h2 id="blog-front-matter"><a class="anchor" href="#blog-front-matter">#</a>Blog post</h2>
<pre>---
title: A 3D home from a street address
date: 2026-11-19
slug: home-3d-from-an-address
summary: What goes in, what comes out, and how far one house got.
---</pre>
  <p>URL is <code>/blog/{date}-{slug}/</code>. Edit the post forever; the URL never changes. The build keeps a list of every URL it has published, and fails if one disappears without a redirect line, so a stray edit to <code>slug</code> or <code>date</code> can't break old links.</p>

  <h2 id="project-front-matter"><a class="anchor" href="#project-front-matter">#</a>Project</h2>
<pre>---
title: Home 3D
status: active            # active · paused · done
summary: A 3D model of a house from its street address, for planning a remodel.
started: 2026-10
posts:
  - "&#91;&#91;home-3d-from-an-address&#93;&#93;"
notes:
  - "&#91;&#91;plan-fit&#93;&#93;"
redirect_from: [address-to-3d-home]
---
The living summary, written freely, with &#91;&#91;wikilinks&#93;&#93; anywhere.</pre>
  <p>The project lists its posts; posts never name their project. So "Appears in" on a post is computed from these lists, and reshuffling or merging projects only touches project files. <code>redirect_from</code> becomes <code>_redirects</code> lines. "Updated" is the newest listed post or the project file's last edit, whichever is later.</p>

  <h2 id="note-front-matter"><a class="anchor" href="#note-front-matter">#</a>Note</h2>
<pre>---
title: Fitting a floor plan to the footprint
summary: Scale, rotate and anchor a drawn plan onto measured geometry.
slug: plan-fit             # optional, defaults to the filename
---</pre>
  <p>No date. "Last tended" is the file's last change. Backlinks are every page that wikilinks here, with the sentence around the link.</p>

  <h2 id="breaking-out"><a class="anchor" href="#breaking-out">#</a>Breaking a note out of a post</h2>
  <p>Start by linking a section: <code>&#91;&#91;home-3d-from-an-address#Fitting the plan to the footprint&#93;&#93;</code> renders as a link to that heading's anchor. When a piece earns its own page, copy it to <code>notes/</code>, point the links at the note, and leave one line in the post (as in the mockup) so the post stays a faithful record. Heading anchors come from the heading text; if you rename a heading that something links to, the build fails and names the link.</p>

  <h2 id="custom-html"><a class="anchor" href="#custom-html">#</a>Custom HTML and visualizations</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>How</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Raw HTML right in the markdown</td><td>A small styled block, an SVG, a few lines of script.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>!&#91;&#91;home-3d-viewer.html&#93;&#93;</code>, a self-contained file in <code>assets/</code></td><td>The default for real visualizations. It runs in a sandboxed frame inside the normal page, so its CSS and JS can't touch the site, and it's easy to build and test on its own. The blog mockup shows one.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>layout: raw</code>, or a page written as <code>.html</code></td><td>A whole page that is the visualization. It skips the site template but keeps its URL, its spot on project pages, and its backlinks.</td></tr>
  </table>
